module

public import WeilConjectures.RingTheory.IsRegularSystemOfParameters

import Mathlib.RingTheory.LocalRing.Module
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Basis.VectorSpace

/-!
# Simultaneous regular parameters for a boundary-compatible flag

Source: Illusie--Laszlo--Orgogozo, Travaux de Gabber, VIII Lemma 4.2.9,
printed p.121, first sentence of proof; Stacks Project, Tag 00NR
(blueprint: lem-per-boundary-regular-flag-parameters).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace IsLocalRing

/-- Two nested ideals separately adapted to fixed boundary parameters admit one adapted
regular system with both ideals generated by initial segments. Source: ILO, VIII Lemma 4.2.9,
printed p.121, first sentence of proof; Stacks Project, Tag 00NR
(blueprint: lem-per-boundary-regular-flag-parameters).

Proof:
1. Put k = R/m and V = m/m². By lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-cotangent-dimension,
   dim_k V = dim R. Rescale each separately adapted parameter system to contain the
   boundary elements exactly. Its classes are a basis of V, since it generates m and has
   dim R elements (def-weil-pbc-regular-system-of-parameters). The boundary classes E
   are independent. For L = P,Q let U_L = (L + m²)/m²; its adapted generators span U_L.
2. In the adapted basis, an element of L in m² has all its generator coefficients in m.
   Thus L/mL injects into V with image U_L. Also a boundary class belongs to U_L iff its
   boundary element belongs to L, and span(E) intersect U_L = span(E intersect U_L).
   The inclusions P ≤ Q give U_P ≤ U_Q and the corresponding inclusions of boundary sets.
3. Extend E intersect U_P to a basis A of U_P. The union of A with the boundary classes
   in U_Q outside U_P is independent: a relation would put their sum in
   span(E) intersect U_P, forcing those coefficients, and then all coefficients, to vanish.
   Extend that union to a basis C of U_Q. The same argument makes C union (E outside U_Q)
   independent in V; extend it to a basis T of V. Use
   lem-weil-pbc-vector-space-basis-through-vector at each extension. Then A ⊆ C ⊆ T
   and E ⊆ T.
4. Lift A to P, C outside A to Q, and T outside C to m, using the actual b elements as
   their own lifts. Step 2 ensures these choices lie in the required ideals. Their classes
   give bases of P/mP, Q/mQ, and m/m². The separately adapted systems already make P,Q
   finite modules, and m is finite by Noetherianity. Apply
   lem-weil-pbc-nakayama-local-generators to obtain generators of P,Q,m respectively.
5. Enumerate T with A first, C outside A next, and T outside C last. With m = |A| and
   l = |C| the first two prefixes generate P,Q. The full list generates the maximal ideal
   and has length dim R, so is a regular system of parameters. Its boundary members are
   exactly the prescribed ones, hence in particular agree up to units. Empty sets and equal
   subspaces cover P = Q, P = 0, empty boundary, and dimension zero.
-/
theorem exists_boundary_regular_flag_parameters
    {R : Type u} [CommRing R] [IsRegularLocalRing R]
    (b : List R) (hb : b.Nodup)
    (hbparams : ∃ s : List R, IsRegularSystemOfParameters s ∧ b ⊆ s)
    (P Q : Ideal R) (hPQ : P ≤ Q) (hQ : Q ≠ ⊤)
    (hPregular : IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ P))
    (hQregular : IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ Q))
    (hadapted : ∀ L : Ideal R, L = P ∨ L = Q →
      ∃ s : List R, IsRegularSystemOfParameters s ∧
        (∀ x ∈ b, ∃ y ∈ s, ∃ a : Rˣ, x = (a : R) * y) ∧
        ∃ S : Set R, S ⊆ {x | x ∈ s} ∧ L = Ideal.span S) :
    ∃ t : List R, IsRegularSystemOfParameters t ∧
      (∀ x ∈ b, ∃ y ∈ t, ∃ a : Rˣ, x = (a : R) * y) ∧
      ∃ m l : ℕ, m ≤ l ∧ l ≤ t.length ∧
        P = Ideal.span {x | x ∈ t.take m} ∧
        Q = Ideal.span {x | x ∈ t.take l} := by
  sorry

end IsLocalRing
